<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaisalmer State</h1><p class="st-meta">1156 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Jaisalmer</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Bhati Rajput desert kingdom of the Thar, whose golden sandstone fort and merchant mansions grew rich on the caravan trade between India and Central Asia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bhati <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> had ruled from earlier capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodurva">Lodurva</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rawal-jaisal">Rawal Jaisal</a> founded the fort and city of Jaisalmer on the Trikuta hill in 1156.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jaisalmer lay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, and merchants paid for elaborate havelis such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patwon-ki-haveli">Patwon ki Haveli</a> and for Jain temples inside the fort. The fort suffered sieges by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in the 13th and 14th centuries. Later the Rawals accepted Mughal overlordship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Jaisalmer signed a treaty with the British in 1818. The decline of caravan trade and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay's</a> sea trade impoverished it in the 19th century. It merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">Greater Rajasthan</a> in 1949, and the fort, still lived in, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaisalmer-state.jpg" alt="Jaisalmer State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Harshvardhan Singh Rawlot ‍(हर्षवर्धन सिंह रावलोत) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaisalmer_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1156 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaisalmer</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rawal Jaisal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marwari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jaisalmer</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1552&amp;focus=jaisalmer-state" aria-label="Jaisalmer State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6547 1190 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaisalmer State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6867 1446L7018 1385M6867 1446L7364 1601M6867 1446L7364 1602M6867 1446L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6867 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6867 1446h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jaisalmer. <a href="/atlas?year=1552&amp;focus=jaisalmer-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
